{"cuisine/nagoya-cuisine":{"t":"cuisine","r":"cuisine/nagoya-cuisine","n":"Nagoya cuisine","c":"#f0b340","crumb":[["Asia","cuisines#asia"],["Japanese regional cuisine","cuisine/japanese-regional-cuisine"]],"sub":"Regional cuisine in Japan","intro":[["Nagoya cuisine is a Japanese regional cuisine of the city of Nagoya and surrounding region in Central Japan. Due to differences in culture, historical contact with other regions, climate, and availability of ingredients, Nagoya cuisine has a distinctive style and unique aspects. Although many dishes derive from local tradition, Nagoya cuisine has also been inspired by foreign cuisines such as Taiwanese cuisine and Italian cuisine. Seasonings such as hatchō miso, mame miso, or tamari (a type of soy sauce produced mainly in the region) characterize the representative foods of Nagoya cuisine."],["Nagoya cuisine can be classified into five main categories:"],["Miso-based (misokatsu, etc.)"],["Chicken-based (tebasaki, etc.)"],["Italian-style (ankake spaghetti, etc.)"],["Asian-style (Taiwan ramen, etc.)"],["Cafe foods (ogura toast, etc.)"],["Nagoya kōchin chicken has been a traditional ingredient of Nagoya cuisine since prewar times. Shrimp – especially fried shrimp, \"ebi fry\", pronounced ebi furyā (エビフリャー) in Nagoya dialect – is another specialty, and is the \"official marine life symbol\" of the prefecture. Dishes inspired by foreign food, including various local spaghetti dishes and \"Taiwanese\" noodles, have become an increasingly significant part of Nagoya cuisine."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"nagoya-cuisine"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Nagoya_cuisine"}],"sections":[{"h":"Recipe sites","count":1,"rows":[{"name":"Just One Cookbook","ref":"site/justonecookbook-com","color":"#55b8ad","shape":"site","sub":"justonecookbook.com","note":"Just One Cookbook's guide to Nagoya specialties such as miso katsu and tebasaki.","url":"https://www.justonecookbook.com/nagoya-food","suggested":true}]},{"h":"Part of","links":[["Japanese regional cuisine","cuisine/japanese-regional-cuisine"]]}],"cont":"Asian cuisine"},"cuisine/javanese-cuisine":{"t":"cuisine","r":"cuisine/javanese-cuisine","n":"Javanese cuisine","c":"#f0b340","crumb":[["Asia","cuisines#asia"],["Indonesian cuisine","cuisine/indonesian-cuisine"]],"sub":"Regional cuisine in Indonesia","intro":[["Javanese cuisine is the cuisine of Javanese people, a major ethnic group in Indonesia in the provinces of Central Java, Yogyakarta, and East Java."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"javanese-cuisine"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Javanese_cuisine"}],"sections":[{"h":"Recipe sites","empty":"None yet","tryLinks":[["Indonesian cuisine","cuisine/indonesian-cuisine"]]},{"h":"Dishes","count":6,"links":[["Buntil","dish/buntil"],["Getuk goreng","dish/getuk-goreng"],["Javanese doughnut","dish/javanese-doughnut"],["Megono","dish/megono"],["Nasi ambeng","dish/nasi-ambeng"],["Nasi gandul","dish/nasi-gandul"]]},{"h":"Key ingredients","links":[["Cassava","ingredient/cassava"],["Coconut milk","ingredient/coconut-milk"],["Coconut","ingredient/coconut"],["Palm sugar","ingredient/palm-sugar"],["Chili pepper","ingredient/chili-pepper"],["Anchovy","ingredient/anchovy"],["Dried fish","ingredient/dried-fish"],["Engraulis","ingredient/engraulis"],["Papaya","ingredient/papaya"],["Taro","ingredient/taro"],["Rice flour","ingredient/rice-flour"],["Vanilla","ingredient/vanilla"],["Grated coconut","ingredient/grated-coconut"],["Rice","ingredient/rice"],["Perkedel","ingredient/perkedel"],["Rempeyek","ingredient/rempeyek"],["Rendang","ingredient/rendang"],["Sambal","ingredient/sambal"],["Tempeh","ingredient/tempeh"],["Urap","ingredient/urap"],["Beef","ingredient/beef"],["Candlenut","ingredient/candlenut"],["Caraway seed","ingredient/caraway-seed"],["Coriander","ingredient/coriander"]],"note":"Most used across its dishes."},{"h":"Part of","links":[["Indonesian cuisine","cuisine/indonesian-cuisine"]]},{"h":"Within Javanese cooking","count":3,"links":[["Betawi cuisine","cuisine/betawi-cuisine"],["Madurese cuisine","cuisine/madurese-cuisine"],["Sundanese cuisine","cuisine/sundanese-cuisine"]]}],"cont":"Asian cuisine","img":{"src":"/labs/cuisines/img/c/cb/Donat_jawa.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","w":500,"h":375,"by":"Rahmawati Hasanah","lic":"CC BY-SA 4.0","licUrl":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Donat_jawa.jpg","of":"Javanese doughnut"}},"cuisine/ashkenazi-jewish-cuisine":{"t":"cuisine","r":"cuisine/ashkenazi-jewish-cuisine","n":"Ashkenazi Jewish cuisine","c":"#cf5f87","crumb":[["Europe","cuisines#europe"],["Jewish cuisine","cuisine/jewish-cuisine"]],"sub":"Cuisine of a people","intro":[["Ashkenazi Jewish cuisine is an assortment of cooking traditions that was developed by the Ashkenazi Jews of Central, Eastern, Northwestern and Northern Europe, and their descendants, particularly in the United States and other Western countries."],["Ashkenazi Jewish foods have frequently been unique to Ashkenazi Jewish communities, and they often consist of local ingredients (such as beets, cabbage, and potato). While these ingredients tended to be the same as those in local or neighbouring non-Jewish communities, the preparation methods were very different due to kashrut, which was historically enforced by a law, and a history of limited interaction between Ashkenazi Jews and non-Jews."],["The cuisine is largely based on ingredients that were affordable to the historically poor Ashkenazi Jewish community of Europe, and it is frequently composed of ingredients that were readily available and affordable in the regions and communities of Europe in which Ashkenazi Jews lived. Some ingredients were considered less desirable than other ingredients, such as brisket, chicken liver, and artichokes, among other ingredients, and as a result, these items were rarely used by gentile neighbours of Ashkenazi Jews."],["Meat is ritually slaughtered in the shechita process, and it is also soaked and salted. Meat dishes are a prominent feature of Shabbat, festivals, and celebratory meals. Braised meats such as brisket feature heavily, as do root vegetables such as potatoes, carrots, and parsnips which are used in such dishes as latkes, matzo ball soup, and tzimmes (a braised fruit and vegetable dish which may also contain meat). Cooked, stuffed, and baked vegetables such as stuffed cabbage and, in some regions, stuffed peppers are central to the cuisine."],["Due to the lack of availability of olive oil and other fats which are commonplace in Jewish cooking, rendered fat from leftover poultry skins (gribenes) called schmaltz is used in fleishig (meat) dishes, while butter is traditionally used in milchig (dairy) dishes. Since the advent of mass-produced vegetable oils (particularly in the United States and Canada) such as canola oil, many baked goods have been made with oils rather than butter, to render them pareve."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ashkenazi_Jewish_cuisine"}],"sections":[{"h":"Recipe sites","empty":"None yet","tryLinks":[["Jewish cuisine","cuisine/jewish-cuisine"]]},{"h":"Cookbooks","count":1,"rows":[{"name":"The Gefilte Manifesto: New Recipes for Old World Jewish Foods","ref":"cookbook/the-gefilte-manifesto-new-recipes-for-old-world-jewish-foods","color":"#f0b340","shape":"book","sub":"Jeffrey Yoskowitz, Liz Alpern, 2016","note":"Updated versions of Eastern European Jewish staples from the founders of The Gefilteria.","url":"https://openlibrary.org/isbn/9781250071385","suggested":true}]},{"h":"Dishes","count":2,"links":[["Kugel","dish/kugel"],["Yerushalmi Kugel","dish/yerushalmi-kugel"]]},{"h":"Key ingredients","links":[["Egg","ingredient/egg"],["Noodles","ingredient/noodles"],["Apple","ingredient/apple"],["Challah","ingredient/challah"],["Cornmeal","ingredient/cornmeal"],["Lokshen","ingredient/lokshen"],["Matzah","ingredient/matzah"],["Potato","ingredient/potato"],["Rice","ingredient/rice"],["Black pepper","ingredient/black-pepper"],["Caramel","ingredient/caramel"],["Cinnamon","ingredient/cinnamon"],["Sugar","ingredient/sugar"]],"note":"Most used across its dishes."},{"h":"Part of","links":[["Jewish cuisine","cuisine/jewish-cuisine"]]}],"cont":"European cuisine"},"cuisine/occitan-cuisine":{"t":"cuisine","r":"cuisine/occitan-cuisine","n":"Occitan cuisine","c":"#cf5f87","crumb":[["Europe","cuisines#europe"],["French cuisine","cuisine/french-cuisine"]],"sub":"Regional cuisine in France","intro":[["Occitan cuisine is the traditional cuisine and gastronomy of Occitania, the supranational region of southern Europe where Occitan is traditionally spoken."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"occitan-cuisine"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Occitan_cuisine"}],"sections":[{"h":"Recipe sites","empty":"None yet","tryLinks":[["French cuisine","cuisine/french-cuisine"]]},{"h":"Cookbooks","count":1,"rows":[{"name":"The Cooking of Southwest France","ref":"cookbook/the-cooking-of-southwest-france","color":"#f0b340","shape":"book","sub":"Paula Wolfert, 1983","note":"Classic study of the rustic cooking of Gascony, Languedoc and the wider Occitan south, including cassoulet.","url":"https://openroadmedia.com/ebook/the-cooking-of-southwest-france/9780544186019","suggested":true}]},{"h":"Part of","links":[["French cuisine","cuisine/french-cuisine"]]}],"cont":"European cuisine"},"dish/acquacotta":{"t":"dish","r":"dish/acquacotta","n":"Acquacotta","c":"#ede6da","crumb":[["Dishes","dishes"]],"sub":"Dish from Italian cuisine","dot":"#cf5f87","intro":[["Acquacotta is an Italian broth-based bread soup that was originally a peasant food. Its preparation and consumption dates back to ancient history, and it originated in the coastal area known as the Maremma, in southern Tuscany and northern Lazio. The dish was invented in part as a means to make hardened, stale bread edible. In contemporary times, ingredients can vary, and additional ingredients are sometimes used. Variations of the dish include acquacotta con funghi and acquacotta con peperoni."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"dish-acquacotta"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Acquacotta"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's from","links":[["Italian cuisine","cuisine/italian-cuisine"]]},{"h":"Cook it","rows":[{"name":"Pasta Grannies","ref":"site/pastagrannies-substack-com","color":"#55b8ad","shape":"site","sub":"pastagrannies.substack.com","note":"Vicky Bennison's long-running video series filming Italian grandmothers making regional pasta by hand.","url":"https://pastagrannies.substack.com/p/pasta-grannies-is-now-on-substack","suggested":true},{"name":"Recipes from Italy","ref":"site/recipesfromitaly-com","color":"#55b8ad","shape":"site","sub":"recipesfromitaly.com","note":"Traditional Italian recipes written by Italian home cooks, organized by region and course.","url":"https://recipesfromitaly.com","suggested":true},{"name":"Manu's Menu","ref":"site/manusmenu-com","color":"#55b8ad","shape":"site","sub":"manusmenu.com","note":"Italian-born home cook Manuela sharing classic family and regional Italian dishes.","url":"https://www.manusmenu.com/about/about-this-site","suggested":true}]},{"h":"More from Italian cuisine","links":[["8½ Otto e Mezzo Bombana","dish/8-otto-e-mezzo-bombana"],["A16 (restaurant)","dish/a16-restaurant"],["Agliata","dish/agliata"],["Agnolotti","dish/agnolotti"],["Alfredo alla Scrofa","dish/alfredo-alla-scrofa"],["Alioto's","dish/alioto-s"],["Amalfi's Italian Restaurant","dish/amalfi-s-italian-restaurant"],["Amato's","dish/amato-s"],["Angelo's Civita Farnese","dish/angelo-s-civita-farnese"],["Antica Pizzeria Port'Alba","dish/antica-pizzeria-port-alba"],["Antico Pizza","dish/antico-pizza"],["Antipasto","dish/antipasto"],["Arancini","dish/arancini"],["ASK